<h1>Matrix Lambda Dimensions</h1>
<p>Some values only exist when the test is configured: the releases currently published, the devices currently
	attached, the branches currently open. A <strong>lambda dimension</strong> takes its values from a function the
	test supplies, and the matrix is built from whatever that function returns. This page follows
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/">Matrix Dimensions Randomization</a> and
	uses the <strong>Matrix Lambda Dimensions</strong> test of the <strong>Advanced Configuration Parameters</strong>
	group.</p>
<p>Select the <strong>Matrix Lambda Dimensions</strong> test:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-lambda-dimensions/matrix-lambda-dimensions-config.png", "Matrix Lambda Dimensions run panel reporting Total Cases 18, with the Maze App Version column header marked with a lambda icon and listing maze-v3.0.0, maze-v2.0.0 and maze-v1.0.0 across the cases", "large") }}
<p>The panel reports <strong>Total Cases: 18</strong>. The <strong>Maze App Version</strong> column header carries a
	lambda icon, and its values, <code>maze-v3.0.0</code>, <code>maze-v2.0.0</code> and <code>maze-v1.0.0</code>, are
	the release tags the test's function fetched from GitHub while the panel was being built. There is no
	<strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> field this time: every case obtains the binary of its own version, so the
	test needs nothing configured before it runs.</p>

<h2 id="defining-a-lambda-dimension">Defining a lambda dimension</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_matrix_lambda_dimensions/") }}
<p>The dimension is declared with
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/#dimensions"><code>Dimension.lambda_choice</code></a>, whose
	<code>factory</code> is a function of no arguments that returns the values:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('matrix = OrcraftConfigField.Matrix(
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension.lambda_choice(label="Maze App Version", factory=lambda: fetch_maze_app_release_versions()),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension(label="command", values=("generate", "solve", "save")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension(label="algorithm", values=(None, "BFS", "DFS", "ASTAR")),

	exclude=[
		lambda case: case.params["command"] in ("generate", "save") and case.params["algorithm"] is not None,
	],
)') }}
<p>The factory runs when the test is configured, which is when the controller builds its run panel, not when the
	test file is imported. Its result becomes the dimension's value list, in the order returned, and the matrix is
	the product of that list with the two listed dimensions. Three releases, three commands and four algorithms give
	36 combinations; the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-cases-exclusion/">exclusion rule</a> removes
	the <code>generate</code> and <code>save</code> cases that name an algorithm, leaving 18.</p>
<p>The label can be any text, and it is the key under which the value reaches the test:
	<code>case.params["Maze App Version"]</code>. The function here is the same GitHub helper that
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/#versions-provider">Lambda Configuration
	Parameters</a> uses for its selector fields. The difference is what the values are for. A
	<code>SelectLambda</code> field offers them for the user to pick one; a lambda dimension turns every one of them
	into cases.</p>

<h3 id="using-the-fetched-values">Using the fetched values</h3>
<p>Each case receives one release tag and resolves it to a folder holding that release's binary:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	bin_folder = get_maze_app_bin_folder(case.params["Maze App Version"])

	maze_app = MazeApp(bin_folder, case.params["command"], algorithm=case.params["algorithm"])

	maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p><code>get_maze_app_bin_folder</code> lives in the suite's shared <code>github_repo_access</code> module. It
	downloads the release asset for the current platform into a folder under the system's temporary directory and
	returns that folder, or returns it at once when the binary is already there. The first run of a version therefore
	downloads it, and every later case of that version reuses the copy.</p>

<h2 id="the-fetched-value-list">The fetched value list</h2>
<p>Click the <strong>Maze App Version</strong> column header. The <strong>Dimension Filter</strong> dialog lists
	the values the factory returned:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-lambda-dimensions/lambda-dimension-dialog.png", "Dimension Filter dialog for the Maze App Version dimension listing maze-v1.0.0, maze-v2.0.0 and maze-v3.0.0, all checked, with Select All and Clear All enabled", "large") }}
<p>The values are checkable, and <strong>Select All</strong> and <strong>Clear All</strong> work as they do for a
	listed dimension, so the matrix can be narrowed to one release for a configuration in the way
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/">Filtering Matrix Dimensions</a> describes.
	There is no <strong>Random Configuration</strong> group and no <strong>Add value</strong> field: the list is what
	the function returned, and a value that is not a published release could not be run.</p>

<h2 id="running-across-releases">Running across releases</h2>
<p>Click <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>. Nothing had to be
	configured, so <strong>Update Plan</strong> stayed disabled and the run executes the plan as loaded. The test
	reports 18 planned, done and passed cases, and the log's result table shows which release each case ran:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-lambda-dimensions/running-matrix-lambda-dimensions-test.png", "Completed Matrix Lambda Dimensions run with all 18 cases passing and the log listing the maze-v2.0.0 and maze-v1.0.0 cases with their command and algorithm", "large") }}
<p>The cases run version by version, newest first, because that is the order the factory returned. Each case's log
	states whether its binary was downloaded or taken from the cache before the Maze application's own output.</p>

<h3 id="inspecting-the-run">Inspecting the run</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the completed run. The <strong>List Dimensions</strong> table records the
	three release tags as the dimension's default, planned and run values, next to the listed commands and
	algorithms:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-lambda-dimensions/matrix-lambda-dimensions-details.png", "Matrix Lambda Dimensions Details view listing maze-v3.0.0, maze-v2.0.0 and maze-v1.0.0 as the Maze App Version values for the default, plan and run, with 18 planned and run cases all passing", "large") }}
<p>This is the record that matters for a dimension whose values change over time. When a fourth release is
	published, the next configuration of the test fetches four tags and plans 24 cases, and the details of this run
	still show the three it covered.</p>

<p>This completes the matrix pages of the tutorial. The next group,
	<strong>Statuses and Results</strong>, begins with
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/orcraft-statuses/">Orcraft Statuses</a>: how a test tells the controller
	that a case passed, passed with a warning, failed or could not run at all.</p>
